\name{sdrf_assess}
\alias{sdrf_assess}
\title{Decide whether an SDRF file describes its samples, or is a valid skeleton}
\usage{
sdrf_assess(path, timeout = 60)
}
\arguments{
\item{path}{Path to a \code{.sdrf.tsv} file.}

\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow, or \code{NULL} to wait indefinitely.
}

}
\description{
Decide whether each SDRF file's sample half describes an experimental design - Informative, Partial or Skeleton - with the per-column counts behind the verdict.

A file generated from a list of data files - reserved words in every sample column, one
replicate number, no factor - passes \code{\link{sdrf_validate}}, because reserved words are the
specification's correct way to say nothing. For grouping results by biology it is the same as
having no SDRF at all. mzLib's \code{SdrfSampleInformativeness.Assess} is the gate for that.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_sdrf_assessment}: \code{path}; \code{verdict} - \code{"Informative"}, \code{"Partial"} or
  \code{"Skeleton"}; the three checks \code{factor_value_varies}, \code{sample_is_described} and
  \code{biological_replicate_varies}; \code{row_count} data rows; \code{caveats}; \code{column_names}; and
  \code{records}, the evidence - one row per column a check read: \code{role}, \code{column_name}, \code{rows} (data
  rows), \code{filled} (rows with a real answer), \code{absent} (rows empty or a reserved word),
  \code{distinct_values} (values, compared ignoring case and surrounding space) and \code{fill_rate}, the
  fraction (0-1) \code{filled / rows}.

}
\section{The verdict is how many checks pass}{


All three is \code{Informative}, none is \code{Skeleton}, anything between is \code{Partial}.
\code{factor_value_varies}: some \code{factor value[...]} column holds two or more real answers.
\code{sample_is_described}: some \code{characteristics[...]} column other than organism and biological
replicate holds a real answer - organism is left out because a search can fill it without a
human. \code{biological_replicate_varies}: \code{characteristics[biological replicate]} holds two or more
real answers; \code{FALSE} also when the column is missing. \code{Partial} is often legitimate - read the
\code{caveats}.

}
